"comparison": "Both JPMorgan and Bank of America define strategic risk specifically through the lens of failed or poorly designed business plans and the inability to respond to external changes. JPMorgan defines it as 'risk to earnings, capital, liquidity or reputation associated with poorly-designed or failed business plans or an inadequate response to changes in the operating environment,' which aligns with Bank of America’s definition resulting from 'inappropriate business plans, ineffective business strategy execution, or failure to respond... to changes in the regulatory, macroeconomic or competitive environments.' A key similarity is the explicit focus on the failure of execution and planning, whereas Bank of America uniquely highlights 'incorrect assumptions' and specific technology developments like AI as drivers of this risk.",
