"comparison": "Both Bank of America and Morgan Stanley categorize strategic risk as a principal risk type inherent to their business activities. Bank of America describes it as a 'major risk category along with credit, market, liquidity, compliance, operational and reputational risks.' Similarly, Morgan Stanley lists 'strategic and reputational risks' among its 'principal risks involved in our business activities' that are both financial and non-financial. Morgan Stanley further specifies that strategic risk is 'integrated into our business planning,' mirroring Bank of America’s description of strategic risk being 'embedded in every business.' Unlike Bank of America, which details specific causes like AI and customer preference changes, Morgan Stanley focuses on the governance aspect, noting that strategic risk is overseen by the Board.",
