"relationship": "same_topic",
"comparison": "Both Bank of America and Citigroup address the definition of strategic risk, but they differ significantly in specificity and impact measurement. Bank of America defines strategic risk qualitatively as resulting from 'incorrect assumptions... inappropriate business plans... or failure to respond... to changes' in the environment, including specific factors like AI. In contrast, Citigroup provides a more quantitative and outcome-focused definition, describing strategic risk as 'the risk of a sustained impact (not episodic impact) to Citi's core strategic objectives as measured by impacts on anticipated earnings, market capitalization or capital.' While both acknowledge 'external factors' and 'strategy execution' risks, Citigroup explicitly distinguishes strategic risk from episodic impacts, whereas Bank of America focuses on the causal origins (assumptions, plans, execution) of the risk.",
